@* A markdown field shown twice: the source in a text area, and beside it the text as it will be
   rendered. The preview follows a local draft of the text, so it keeps up with every character typed
   or deleted without the model hearing about it.

   The edited text is handed back when the field is left, not on each keystroke, because saving is
   what the caller does with it and saving serialises the whole plan — far more work than a single
   character warrants. That is also what a plain @bind on a text area does, so callers can keep
   using @bind-Value:after as before.

   Rendered with the same Markdig call the printed reports use, so what the preview shows is what
   the print gets — give it the print's own class through PreviewClass and the styling matches too. *@
<div class="markdown-editor">
    <textarea rows="@Rows" value="@_draft"
              @oninput="e => _draft = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty"
              @onchange="Commit"></textarea>
    <div class="preview @PreviewClass">@Rendered</div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The markdown source as stored.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Value { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>Raised with the edited markdown when the text area is left.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string> ValueChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Visible lines of the text area, which is what gives both panes their height.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int Rows { get; set; } = 12;

    /// <summary>Optional class for the preview pane, normally the class the print uses for the same
    /// text, so the preview is styled the way it will be printed.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? PreviewClass { get; set; }

    private string _draft = "";
    private string _lastValue = "";

    private MarkupString Rendered => new(Markdig.Markdown.ToHtml(_draft));

    // Anything else that re-renders the page (a docked view refreshing, another edit being saved)
    // hands the same value back while typing is still in progress; only a value that has genuinely
    // changed elsewhere may replace the draft.
    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        if (Value == _lastValue) return;
        _lastValue = Value;
        _draft = Value;
    }

    private async Task Commit(ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        _draft = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty;
        if (_draft == Value) return;
        // Remember what was sent, so the value coming back from the caller is not mistaken for an
        // edit made elsewhere.
        _lastValue = _draft;
        await ValueChanged.InvokeAsync(_draft);
    }
}
